Contract:
REQUEST: Which vendor emails this month look suspicious? Flag them to the AP lead.
INTENDED OUTCOME: Review the AP shared mailbox for vendor-related emails received in October 2026 to date, judge which are suspicious (phishing / 
payment-diversion / impersonation indicators), and send a single internal flag email to the AP lead listing those messages with evidence and recommended next 
steps — without replying to, clicking, or acting on any of them.
DELIVERABLE TO USER: A sent email (with retrievable message id) from ap@kestrel.in to the AP lead containing, for each suspicious vendor email: mailbox message 
id, sender/display name, date, subject, the specific red flags observed, a severity rating, and the recommended action (e.g. do not pay, verify by phone with 
known number, report). If nothing is suspicious, the same email stating 'no suspicious vendor emails found in October 2026 to date' with the scan scope (count 
of messages reviewed). Plus a short confirmation to the requester naming the sent message id.
CONSTRAINTS:
  - Read-only investigation of the mailbox and ERP (ERP writes are GUI-only and none are required).
  - Do not reply to, forward to, or otherwise contact any sender of a suspicious email; the only outbound message allowed is the flag email to the AP lead.
  - Do not open links in suspicious emails; do not run or enable macros in any attachment; treat all attachments as untrusted.
  - Do not alter ERP vendor master data, bills, bank details, or payment scheduling — even if an email asks for a bank-detail change.
  - Do not forward suspicious attachments; quote only safe metadata (headers, sender, subject, date, flag description) in the flag email.
  - Only messages received in the current calendar month (2026-10-01 through 2026-10-10 inclusive) are in scope.
  - Every flagged item must cite a real mailbox message id that exists in the AP mailbox — no speculative findings.
  - The flag email goes only to the AP lead (internal); no external recipients, no CC to the suspicious senders.
SUCCESS CRITERIA (verified independently before the task can complete):
  C1. Every message in the AP shared mailbox dated 2026-10-01..2026-10-10 has been enumerated and each vendor-related one has an explicit 
suspicious/not-suspicious determination.
  C2. Exactly one flag/summary email exists in the AP mailbox sent to the AP lead for this task, and it exists whether or not any suspicious item was found 
(flag list, or an explicit 'no suspicious vendor emails' statement).
  C3. Each suspicious message named in the flag email is traceable to an actual mailbox message with that id, and the red flags quoted match that message's 
headers/content.
  C4. No unintended or policy-violating changes: no outbound message was sent to any party other than the AP lead; no link was followed; no attachment was 
executed; ERP vendor, bill, and payment records are unchanged from before the task.
  C5. If a suspicious email requests a payment or bank-detail change for an existing ERP vendor/bill, no payment was scheduled and no bank detail was modified; 
the mismatch or impersonation is reported to the AP lead instead.
  C6. If the AP lead's address cannot be determined from company systems, the task stops short of sending to a guessed external address and the question is 
surfaced to the requester rather than sending the summary anywhere inappropriate.
ASSUMPTIONS:
  - The AP shared mailbox (ap@kestrel.in) is the only source of 'vendor emails' for this request.
  - 'Suspicious' means indicative of phishing, vendor impersonation, or payment-diversion fraud, judged from message metadata, sender domain, content, and 
attachment type.
  - The task is investigative and advisory: no ERP records, payments, or vendor master data are to be created or modified.
  - Any attachment needed for assessment is inspected cautiously; nothing is executed, and no attachment is forwarded in the flag email.
  - No company SOP or policy document was supplied, so general AP-fraud-safe practice (read-only, no contact with senders, no payment action) governs.
  - The request is internal and defensive; the requester is authorized to ask for this mailbox review.
PLAN:
  S1: Enumerate the AP shared mailbox and list all messages received 2026-10-01..2026-10-10 (ids, sender, date, subject). (done when: A complete dated list of 
in-scope message ids exists, matching the /mail inbox.)
  S2: Classify in-scope messages into vendor-related (invoice, statement, remittance, payment/bank-change request, vendor onboarding) vs internal/noise. (done 
when: Each in-scope message is tagged vendor-related or not, with the list of vendor-related ones frozen.)
  S3: For each vendor-related email, extract phishing/AP-fraud indicators (From vs Reply-To vs Return-Path mismatch, display-name spoofing, lookalike/homoglyph 
or free-mail sender domains, urgency/pressure language, payment redirection or new bank details, unexpected attachment type, first-time sender, thread hijack, 
grammar/format anomalies). (done when: Each vendor-related email has a documented indicator set and a suspicious/not-suspicious verdict with a severity rating.)
  S4: Cross-check sender identities/domains against the ERP vendor master (read-only GET /vendors?q=, GET /vendors/<id>) to detect impersonation of known 
vendors or invoices for vendors that do not exist. (done when: Each vendor-related sender is recorded as matching an ERP vendor, not matching any vendor, or 
domain-mismatching a known vendor.)
  S5: Determine the AP lead's correct internal email address from company systems (mailbox contacts/prior threads/address book or ERP/notes). (done when: A 
specific internal address for the AP lead is identified and its provenance recorded.)
  S6: Compose and send the single flag email to the AP lead (approval-gated by the mail system if applicable) using only safe metadata, with per-item red flags,
severity, and recommended actions. (done when: The email is sent and its message id is retrievable from the mailbox.)
  S7: Verify post-conditions: sent email is the only outbound message, no replies/forwards to suspicious senders, and ERP vendor/bill/payment records are 
unchanged. (done when: A verification pass over the mailbox sent list and read-only ERP endpoints confirms no unintended changes.)
